  #line-at(72pt, 81.36pt, "As previously noted, the HX effectiveness was observed to be generally low in the turbulent regime with high pumping ")
  #line-at(72pt, 94.68pt, "requirements based on the pressure drop profiles (cf. Figure 9(b)). On this basis, it may seem desirable to operate the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 108pt, "HX in the laminar flow regime although most air-to-air HX in practical applications operate in the turbulent regime. ")
  #line-at(72pt, 121.44pt, "However, a more important parameter used to quantify the HX heat transfer performance the Nusselt number, ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(526.72pt, 121.44pt, "𝑁𝑢")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.02pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 134.9pt, "evaluated using eqn. xx. The computed Nusselt number for the different inlet temperature combinations and Reynolds ")
  #line-at(72pt, 148.22pt, "number are shown in the error-bar plot of Figure 13(a) for both gyroid and tessellated wavy channel architecture. The ")
  #line-at(72pt, 161.54pt, "Nusselt number abstracts the operating temperatures and fluid type from the quantification of the heat transfer ")
  #line-at(72pt, 174.86pt, "efficiency. On this basis, the average Nusselt number is observed to increase almost linearly with increasing Reynolds ")
  #line-at(72pt, 188.18pt, "number which indicates an overall increase in the heat transfer coefficients in both architectures. Linear correlation ")
  #line-at(72pt, 201.5pt, "trends based on regression fitting of the computed datapoints to the Seider-Tate type equation (cf. eqn. (11)) appear in ")
  #line-at(72pt, 214.82pt, "Figure 13(a) for the tessellated wavy channel structure (red dashed lined) and the gyroid structure (black dashed line) ")
